# Copy to .env only if you need to change something. THE DEFAULTS WORK.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LEAVE VITE_API_BASE UNSET. This is the important line in this file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The browser talks only to Vite's own origin: api/client.ts defaults API_BASE
# to the relative '/api', and vite.config.ts forwards that to the backend. One
# origin, so there is no preflight, no CORS header to get rewritten in transit,
# and no localhost-vs-127.0.0.1 / IPv4-vs-IPv6 mismatch to fall into.
#
# Setting VITE_API_BASE to an absolute URL OPTS OUT of all of that — it goes
# straight to the backend cross-origin and the proxy stops being involved.
# That is correct for a deployed backend and wrong for local development, and
# it has already cost real debugging time twice: once as an IPv6 lookup
# failure that silently served fixtures while looking live, and once as a
# proxy that appeared broken because nothing was going through it.
#
#   VITE_API_BASE=https://api.example.com     # deployed backend ONLY

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VITE_PROXY_TARGET — where Vite forwards /api. Change THIS, not the above.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Default is http://127.0.0.1:8001. Set it if your backend runs elsewhere.
#
# PORT 8001, not 8000: 8000 is the default for uvicorn, FastAPI and Django, so
# it is the most contested port on a developer machine. When something else
# holds it, this app's requests reach a stranger's API and come back 404 rather
# than refusing the connection — a wrong answer, which is slower to spot than
# no answer. The Makefile defaults to 8001 for the same reason, so run the
# backend with `--port 8001` if you start it by hand.
#
# 127.0.0.1, not localhost: where `localhost` resolves to ::1 before 127.0.0.1
# — the macOS default — a lookup gets ::1 and finds nothing, because uvicorn's
# default bind is IPv4-only. `uvicorn --host ::` is NOT the fix and was
# measured: it binds IPv6 only and breaks the IPv4 side instead.
#
# `make dev` sets this for you from BACKEND_HOST/BACKEND_PORT and its value
# wins over this file, so `make dev BACKEND_PORT=8000` still works.
#
#   VITE_PROXY_TARGET=http://127.0.0.1:8000   # if you run uvicorn on its default port

# Earth Engine flood layers are served by the backend, not the browser — no
# frontend key is needed. Configure GEE_PROJECT on the backend instead.
